course: dictionary
lesson: 2720
title: Words that set a scene
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Writers choose words to paint a picture for the reader. Dingy is one of those words. It tells you a place looks dark, dull and dirty, and that it is not nice to be in.
explain: Look at the words around it to be sure. In the Mole's words, «I know it's a—shabby, dingy little place … not like—your cosy quarters—or Toad's beautiful hall—or Badger's great house», the writer sets dingy against cosy, beautiful and great. Those are all good places to be, so dingy must be the opposite: a poor, gloomy place.
explain: Dingy can also describe colours. A dingy colour has faded and lost its brightness, so it looks dull rather than fresh. Use the same check as always: swap your meaning in and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Dingy means dark, dull and dirty-looking; not bright or clean.
fact: Dingy can also describe a colour that has faded and looks dull, not fresh.
fact: Writers use dingy in stories to set a gloomy scene, such as a dingy attic, a dingy cellar or a dingy street.
fact: Shabby is a close partner word: both show that something is worn-down and not nice to look at.
fact: Context clues come from the words around a word, so look at what the writer compares it with.
exercise: «I know it's a—shabby, dingy little place» What does dingy mean in this sentence? | Huge and grand | Bright, clean and sunny | *Dark, dull and dirty-looking | Noisy and full of people
exercise: Which word in the Mole's words means worn-down and not nice? | beautiful | *shabby | cosy | great
exercise: What does a dingy colour look like? | Bright and shiny like a new penny | *Faded and dull, not fresh | Bright red like a fire engine | Glowing in the dark
test: «Toad's prison cell is dark and dingy.» What is the cell like? | A warm room with a bright fire | A clean, sunny room with a view | *A dark, dirty, gloomy room | A big hall full of gold
test: The Mole calls his home dingy, but he says he was fond of it. What does that tell us? | Toad had taken it from him | *He still loved his home, even though it was shabby | He hated it and wanted to leave at once | He was rich and did not care about his home
exam: Why does a writer use the word dingy to describe a place in a story? | To show the place is a grand palace | To tell us the weather is sunny | *To set a gloomy scene and show the place is shabby and unpleasant | To make the reader laugh with a joke
